  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Springs lasted 1.1 innings. The game was decided by inning two.",
    "what_this_means": "The early damage stuck. Minnesota scored three in the first, three more in the second, and the Athletics never caught up despite a steady offensive effort. They scored in six of their ten RISP situations, converting enough runners to stay close. McNeil drove in two, and White contributed from the second spot. But the deficit was too steep from the start. The bullpen steadied things after Springs departed, with Morales throwing 4.2 scoreless innings and striking out five, but clean relief cannot erase a five-run hole after two innings. The Athletics had runners and found the zone, moving the ball for 8 hits and scoring 8 runs, yet early incompetence made every run feel like it came too late.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like from the inside. They did not strike out eight times and give up. They scored in multiple innings, moved runners when they had to, and nearly clawed back from a position that should have been hopeless. But good teams do not surrender the first two innings. The deficit is not the story. The timing is. They had the pieces to win this game and assembled them in the wrong order. Eight left on base is not the failure. Giving Minnesota six in two innings was.",
